Top Git Interview Questions for DevOps Engineers :

1. What is the difference between git fetch and git pull?
👉 git fetch downloads the latest changes from the remote repository but does not merge them into your current branch.
👉 git pull downloads and automatically merges the changes into your current branch.

2. What is the difference between git merge and git rebase?
👉 git merge combines branches and creates a merge commit, preserving branch history.
👉 git rebase moves your commits on top of another branch, creating a cleaner and more linear history.

3. What is a Git Branch?
👉 A branch is an independent line of development that allows developers to work on features or bug fixes without affecting the main codebase.

4. What is a Pull Request (PR)?
👉 A Pull Request is a request to merge code changes from one branch into another. It allows code reviews and approval before merging.

5. What is a Merge Conflict?
👉 A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically determine which changes to keep while merging branches. The conflict must be resolved manually.

6. What is Git Stash?
👉 Git Stash temporarily saves uncommitted changes so you can switch branches or perform other tasks without committing incomplete work.

7. Explain the Git Workflow.
👉 Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository → Remote Repository
This is the basic workflow followed when developing and pushing code changes.

8. What is the difference between git clone and git pull?
👉 git clone creates a complete local copy of a remote repository.
👉 git pull updates an existing local repository with the latest changes from the remote repository.

9. How do you undo the last commit?
👉 git reset --soft HEAD~1 removes the commit but keeps the changes.
👉 git reset --hard HEAD~1 removes both the commit and the changes.
👉 git revert <commit-id> creates a new commit that reverses the previous changes.

10. How does Jenkins trigger a build when code is pushed to Git?
👉 Git repositories can send a webhook notification to Jenkins whenever code is pushed. Jenkins receives the event and automatically triggers the CI/CD pipeline.


